WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.611
In part one, order mattered: the list two

2
00:00:06.611 --> 00:00:09.439
five is not the list five, two.

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:12.620
A set forgets order and repetition.

4
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.458
Think of a set as shading on

5
00:00:15.546 --> 00:00:17.590
the numbers one to ten.

6
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.877
Shade two and five, or five and two

7
00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:22.121
the same set.

8
00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:25.586
Shade five again, and nothing changes

9
00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:27.274
the set two, five

10
00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:29.850
five still has two elements.

11
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.953
Here is a question.

12
00:00:32.045 --> 00:00:34.537
How many subsets does a set

13
00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:36.751
of three elements have?

14
00:00:36.751 --> 00:00:38.320
Pause and count.

15
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.851
A set is a collection of elements

16
00:00:44.851 --> 00:00:47.282
and only membership counts.

17
00:00:47.282 --> 00:00:51.014
We write x in A when x is an element of A.

18
00:00:51.014 --> 00:00:54.660
The even numbers up to ten form a set, E.

19
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:56.570
It has five elements

20
00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:58.740
its cardinality is five.

21
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:02.551
The empty set has no elements at all

22
00:01:02.551 --> 00:01:04.235
cardinality zero.

23
00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:07.603
It is not the same as the set whose

24
00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:10.410
one element is the empty set.

25
00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.522
A is a subset of B when every element

26
00:01:14.618 --> 00:01:17.386
of A is also an element of B.

27
00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:21.205
Take the set whose only element is six.

28
00:01:21.205 --> 00:01:25.310
Six is even, so that set is a subset of E.

29
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.037
Careful with the two symbols.

30
00:01:28.037 --> 00:01:31.235
One is an element of the set one, two.

31
00:01:31.235 --> 00:01:34.678
The set containing one is a subset of it.

32
00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:38.285
But that set is not an element of one, two

33
00:01:38.285 --> 00:01:41.400
the elements of one, two are numbers

34
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.220
not sets.

35
00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.319
And the empty set is a subset of every set

36
00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.848
it has no element that could

37
00:01:48.935 --> 00:01:50.330
fail to be in B.

38
00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.233
The power set of A is the

39
00:01:53.326 --> 00:01:55.463
set of all its subsets.

40
00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:58.809
For the set a, b, c: the empty set

41
00:01:58.809 --> 00:02:02.991
three sets with one element, three with two

42
00:02:02.991 --> 00:02:04.756
and the whole set.

43
00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:06.150
Eight subsets.

44
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.910
A common slip is to forget the

45
00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:12.445
empty set and the whole set, and count six.

46
00:02:12.445 --> 00:02:14.212
But both are subsets

47
00:02:14.212 --> 00:02:16.140
so the answer is eight.

48
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.699
Why eight?

49
00:02:17.797 --> 00:02:20.537
A subset is decided by three

50
00:02:20.635 --> 00:02:22.592
yes or no questions.

51
00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:23.767
Is a in it?

52
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:28.171
Yes for four subsets, no for the other four.

53
00:02:28.171 --> 00:02:29.346
Is b in it?

54
00:02:29.346 --> 00:02:30.520
Is c in it?

55
00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.447
Write the answers as a list

56
00:02:33.543 --> 00:02:37.743
of ones and zeros: the set a, c becomes one

57
00:02:37.743 --> 00:02:38.793
zero, one.

58
00:02:38.793 --> 00:02:41.466
Each subset gives one list

59
00:02:41.466 --> 00:02:44.520
and each list gives one subset.

60
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.956
We counted those lists in part one

61
00:02:47.956 --> 00:02:51.870
two times two times two, which is eight.

62
00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.987
In general, a set with n elements has

63
00:02:56.089 --> 00:02:58.227
two to the n subsets.

64
00:02:58.227 --> 00:03:02.300
And one more element doubles the count

65
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.558
every old subset appears twice

66
00:03:05.558 --> 00:03:09.020
once without it and once with it.

67
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.291
Back to the numbers one to ten.

68
00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:14.636
Let E be the even numbers

69
00:03:14.636 --> 00:03:17.240
and T the multiples of three.

70
00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.336
Their union holds everything in either set

71
00:03:21.336 --> 00:03:22.643
seven numbers.

72
00:03:22.643 --> 00:03:26.302
Their intersection holds what is in both

73
00:03:26.302 --> 00:03:27.173
only six.

74
00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:31.094
So the size of the union is five plus three

75
00:03:31.094 --> 00:03:34.230
minus the one shared number: seven.

76
00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.842
The difference, E minus T

77
00:03:36.929 --> 00:03:39.626
keeps the even numbers that are

78
00:03:39.713 --> 00:03:42.671
not multiples of three: two, four

79
00:03:42.671 --> 00:03:43.975
eight and ten.

80
00:03:44.062 --> 00:03:47.716
The symmetric difference keeps the numbers

81
00:03:47.803 --> 00:03:50.500
in exactly one of the two sets.

82
00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:53.871
Union and intersection don't

83
00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:57.759
care about order, but difference does.

84
00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:00.750
T minus E keeps the multiples

85
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.541
of three that are not even

86
00:04:03.541 --> 00:04:05.635
just three and nine.

87
00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:08.127
So E minus T and T minus

88
00:04:08.227 --> 00:04:10.320
E are different sets.

89
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.215
How do we prove that two sets are equal?

90
00:04:14.215 --> 00:04:17.945
Show that each one is a subset of the other.

91
00:04:17.945 --> 00:04:20.100
This is double inclusion.

92
00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:25.556
Claim: A minus, B union C, equals A minus B

93
00:04:25.556 --> 00:04:28.805
intersected with A minus C.

94
00:04:28.805 --> 00:04:32.170
Take any x on the left side.

95
00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:36.579
Then x is in A, but not in B union C

96
00:04:36.579 --> 00:04:39.828
so x is in neither B nor C.

97
00:04:39.828 --> 00:04:44.469
So x is in A minus B, and in A minus C

98
00:04:44.469 --> 00:04:47.370
it is on the right side.

99
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.414
Now take any x on the right side.

100
00:04:50.414 --> 00:04:53.353
It is in A, not in B, and not in C

101
00:04:53.353 --> 00:04:55.721
so it is not in their union

102
00:04:55.721 --> 00:04:57.680
it is on the left side.

103
00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:59.476
Both inclusions hold

104
00:04:59.476 --> 00:05:01.680
so the two sets are equal.

105
00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:04.357
An example on the board

106
00:05:04.357 --> 00:05:06.684
with A all ten numbers

107
00:05:06.684 --> 00:05:10.756
B the evens and C the multiples of three

108
00:05:10.756 --> 00:05:14.247
both sides are one, five and seven.

109
00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:17.155
That checks the theorem once.

110
00:05:17.155 --> 00:05:19.870
The proof covers every set.

111
00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.233
One example is never a proof

112
00:05:23.233 --> 00:05:27.262
a false law can agree with many examples.

113
00:05:27.262 --> 00:05:29.949
But for a single element x

114
00:05:29.949 --> 00:05:32.347
only three facts matter.

115
00:05:32.347 --> 00:05:33.499
Is it in A?

116
00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:34.074
In B?

117
00:05:34.074 --> 00:05:34.650
In C?

118
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.999
That gives eight cases

119
00:05:36.999 --> 00:05:39.781
the rows of a membership table.

120
00:05:39.781 --> 00:05:43.258
For De Morgan's law, only the row in A

121
00:05:43.258 --> 00:05:46.996
not in B, not in C, puts x on either side

122
00:05:46.996 --> 00:05:48.995
and it puts x on both.

123
00:05:48.995 --> 00:05:52.559
When both sides agree in all eight rows

124
00:05:52.559 --> 00:05:55.080
the law holds for every set.

125
00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.203
Try it yourself

126
00:05:57.203 --> 00:06:01.446
how many subsets of one to six contain one

127
00:06:01.446 --> 00:06:02.700
but not two?

128
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.528
Next time: quantifiers

129
00:06:05.528 --> 00:06:08.330
for all and there exists.
